Z||000002||StuGood||06-26-2000||11:44 PM||||The Haynes manual says there is an engine ID number stamped on the block, "either on front lower left end or front upper left end of the block on all V6 engines."  <P>Chilton's calls (what appears to be) the same number a VIN.  So, if you can find the number on your block and it matches the car's VIN (visible thru winshield, driver's side of car), you probably have the original engine.<P>As far as engine longevity goes, it probably depends a LOT upon how the car has been treated and maintained.<P>For example, my most recent buy was a 200K+ mile, dead Fiero.  Revived it w/o removing heads nor crank nor cam.  It needed timing chain, lifters, oil seals, etc., but now it runs fine, has good compression & doesn't smoke. BUT it's possible the car was babied before I bought it.<P>And if you think your posts are too long, look how windy mine get  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">!      ||||reg||1
